WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.240
- [Announcer] He has won and won big,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.090
in everything he's driven.

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:05.630
- Hey guys, I'm William Byron.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:08.427
I drive the 24 car in
the Nascar Cup series.

00:00:08.427 --> 00:00:11.010
(upbeat music)

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.240
I started racing when I was 15 years old.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.620
And before that, I raced on iRacing,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.020
which is like a computer game basically,

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.430
learned how to drive on there,

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:27.880
and then got started racing real cars

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.611
and have driven ever since.

00:00:29.611 --> 00:00:31.220
(upbeat music)

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.010
Basically just get up eight o'clock, 8:30,

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:34.843
I go train.

00:00:34.843 --> 00:00:36.860
It's funny my trainer,
you know he's just joking,

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.760
he's like, oh yeah you know
you just drive in circles

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:39.710
for a living.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:42.010
And I'm like, it's definitely interesting

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:44.100
because that is what we're
doing, you know actually,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.180
but there's a lot of
training that goes into it.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.850
The training we do is
just, is all physical stuff

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.460
of trying to get your
heart rate really high

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:54.510
and be able to recover.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.770
Cars get to be 140 degrees during the race

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:01.040
and your body really wears
down, and over time from that.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.389
And also, you're holding
on and you're tensing up

00:01:04.389 --> 00:01:06.586
and you're also getting pushed
by the g-forces in the car

00:01:06.586 --> 00:01:08.373
to the right or left.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.390
People ask a lot about like, crashes

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.080
or what's the biggest hit you've had.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.980
Honestly, I had a pretty
big hit last year,

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.530
I hit the wall pretty hard,
got a little airborne.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:18.680
You know, I've never really been injured,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.280
I've just had you know,
soreness, things like that.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.580
Our race cars are pretty safe.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:23.810
The only difference though is like,

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.300
you don't necessarily get
hurt but when you hit a wall

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.420
it feels like a baseball
bat hit you in the stomach.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.150
If you think about getting
hurt, I think it slows you down,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.070
so I think if you block it out completely

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:40.170
and just try to focus on the race.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.510
I think I'm the baby of the series.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.630
We get a lot of age comments like,

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:45.850
oh you look so young.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.010
And you know, I do but
it's like, it's different

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.940
to be so young but I don't
really worry about it.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.403
When I put the helmet
on, it's kind of game on.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.660
I mean I do school work still,

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.000
so I'm still a college student.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.800
So that probably would
surprise some people.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.590
You know, you have to introduce
yourself to the class,

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.230
so I kind of feel weird
talking about, you know,

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.623
I race these cars, so
it's a little different.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.590
Yeah, I was scared of
failing my driving test.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.040
I never parallel parked.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.390
I use two feet when I drive

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.200
and most people you know, use one foot.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.210
But I think it's weird
that people use one foot.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.040
So I was worried about those two things,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.423
I thought I was going
to fail but it was okay.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.220
Thankfully, no, been close.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.970
I got pulled over one
time going like 55 in a 30

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.633
or something, it was a
warning, so it's all good.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.300
My family when we come
home for the holiday's

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.990
and we're all together, they hate the fact

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.350
that I sit in the back right seat

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.690
and I critique everything going on.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:45.820
Especially after the fact,

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:48.430
like oh well you should've done this.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.600
I'm a very like, task-oriented
so it's like everything

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.050
has to be done now.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:58.210
And if something's like put
off, I struggle with that.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:00.030
So it's like I gotta do things

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.680
and then like get done with them.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:03.860
Yeah, they come to some races.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.780
I mean, I try to get my friends
to come out to Charlotte

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.818
and some of the local places that I race.

00:03:09.818 --> 00:03:11.210
So it's always cool to see them.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:13.720
They wear the t-shirts
and look like a fan,

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:14.570
it's pretty cool.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:16.673
Yeah, it is hard.

00:03:16.673 --> 00:03:19.890
I mean most of my friends
are gone during the week

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:24.080
when I'm home, so most of my
friends outside of racing,

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.760
are kind of, you know I
see them once or twice

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:27.720
every few months.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.830
But the people I know in racing,
we kind of become friends

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.780
because we're always seeing each other.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:37.060
Well, I think racing,
it's kind of like golf.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.110
You don't know how difficult it is

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:41.100
til you kinda get out there and do it.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.200
So I feel like, if we could
get people in the car more,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.300
and get them to kind of
drive, or sit next to somebody

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.070
and drive, if we could
do that to a casual fan,

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.595
it would be awesome.

00:03:51.595 --> 00:03:52.840
Because I think it would
help them understand

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:53.673
what's going on.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.020
If I have to go to the
restroom, where do I, you know,

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:58.730
how do I do that during the race.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:00.680
Honestly, like, you get
the adrenaline going

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:02.730
and you don't really
need to use the restroom

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.707
when you're in the car.

00:04:04.707 --> 00:04:07.950
But if you do, like this Nomex is okay.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.617
But try not to.

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:14.200
(upbeat music)

